--TEST--
`Core\Secret::revealBytes` hands back every one of the 256 octets unchanged — half of them ones no `string` can carry — and agrees at every length including the empty one — ADR 0033 § 3
--FILE--
<?nvs
// ADR 0033 § 3 asks for a `bytes` overload and the registry has no
// overloading, so `revealBytes` is a second name — `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/secret.rs`'s
// module doc owns why that rather than a union parameter or a type variable.
// What no existing case pins is that the second name reaches the octets the
// first one cannot: a `revealBytes` that answered through a `string` — a
// round-trip, a re-encode, a normalization — prints exactly the right thing for
// an API token and destroys a signing key, which is the operand this member
// exists for. So the sweep is over the byte values themselves, counted.
uint $v = 0;
int $identical = 0;
int $sameLength = 0;
int $carriedByText = 0;
while ($v < 256) {
    secret bytes $one = Core\Bytes::fill(1, $v);
    bytes $revealed = Core\Secret::revealBytes($one, "the signer takes the key one octet at a time");
    if (Core\Bytes::at($revealed, 0) == $v) {
        $identical = $identical + 1;
    }
    // Length is asked separately from content: a member that padded or
    // terminated its answer still gets octet 0 right.
    if (Core\Bytes::length($revealed) == 1) {
        $sameLength = $sameLength + 1;
    }
    // And this is what makes the first count a claim about `bytes` rather than
    // about text: exactly the octets below 0x80 stand alone as UTF-8, so the
    // other half of the sweep is ground `Core\Secret::reveal` could not have
    // covered at all. Counted rather than asserted in a comment, because the
    // boundary is the whole point of there being two members.
    if (Core\Encoding::isValidText($revealed, Core\Charset::Utf8)) {
        $carriedByText = $carriedByText + 1;
    }
    $v = $v + 1;
}
echo "identical ", $identical, " of 256, one octet long ", $sameLength, "\n";
echo "a string carries ", $carriedByText, ", so ", 256 - $carriedByText, " of them it cannot\n";

// Length is the second axis, swept from the empty buffer up over a pattern
// that is mostly not text — a lone continuation byte, a truncated two-byte
// sequence, `c0` and `ff` which are no UTF-8 at all. Every prefix is revealed
// and compared against the prefix itself, so a member that answered a
// re-encoded copy differs here at the first length it could not carry.
bytes $pattern = Core\Encoding::fromHex("00ff8041c2fe0d0affc0809b7f00e0fd");
secret bytes $held = $pattern;
int $agreed = 0;
int $n = 0;
while ($n <= 16) {
    bytes $prefix = Core\Bytes::slice($pattern, 0, $n);
    secret bytes $secretPrefix = $prefix;
    if (Core\Bytes::compare(Core\Secret::revealBytes($secretPrefix, "the record is written whole"), $prefix) == 0) {
        $agreed = $agreed + 1;
    }
    $n = $n + 1;
}
echo "every length agrees: ", $agreed, " of 17\n";

// The whole buffer once more, printed as the octets it still is: 16 of them,
// unchanged, from a binding that was `secret` a line before.
echo Core\Encoding::toHex(Core\Secret::revealBytes($held, "the audit prints the key it disclosed")), "\n";
--EXPECT--
identical 256 of 256, one octet long 256
a string carries 128, so 128 of them it cannot
every length agrees: 17 of 17
00ff8041c2fe0d0affc0809b7f00e0fd
